* For 0.x

 - Consider adding `rescue` to kick-off a dialog-based rootfs recovery of some
   sort?  Or maybe just add a /bin/rescue script to run via `initramsys` shell?

 - Udev rules to rename network interfaces like systemd, so pre-boot network
   config can actually have the right device names?

   https://systemd.io/PREDICTABLE_INTERFACE_NAMES/
   https://wiki.archlinux.org/title/Network_configuration#Change_interface_name

 - For long-lasting systems, constantly installing updates, doing regular work,
   etc, eventually results in essentially the entire collection of sqsh_layers
   being replaced in upper with new files.  So keeping the original sqsh_layers
   around and overlaying eventually turns into a bit of a resource (cpu and
   space) waste.

   Instead of doing sqsh-commit and sqsh-merge, the user may choose to
   "un-sqsh_layer" back into a "regular" root filesystem.  Perhaps provide
   sqsh_layers2regular and regular2sqsh_layers?

 - Sqsh_layer maintenance scripts for all the things that require rebooting.

   In particular, sqsh-commit (to create new sqsh from upper) and sqsh-merge (to
   merge all lower layers into a single sqsh).  And actually, maint might make
   more sense... since it will have all the layers mounted.  Although, if we
   want to do a full reboot after re-layering (to ensure the bootloader cfg
   changes happens before continuing), that won't work via maint, so initramsys
   would be better suited.

   Would still have to tackle the "now go update your bootloader config"
   dilemma.  Should we sed changes into extlinux.conf, syslinux.conf, or
   reconfigure grub?  Sounds dicey.  I suppose printing out a message telling
   the user EXACTLY what to go put in their bootloader config is the best
   compromise.  Or we could *attempt* to find syslinux.conf and sed changes in
   there if found, otherwise tell the user what to change?  Worst case, they
   don't do it, and their system doesn't boot?

   Should we add kernel params to do this, too?  Like sqsh_layer-commit and
   sqsh_layer-merge?  Have them auto-run and leave the user with a prompt to
   update bootloader config and reboot (if non syslinux), or just go right
   ahead and autoreboot after updating syslinux.conf (ha, don't forget to make
   sure to remove sqsh_layer-commit and sqsh_layer-merge before rebooting)?

 - Hmm... looks like the eudev project is dead.  At some point we'll have to
   start building just the bits of systemd we want (e.g., udev, systemd-boot)
   and firgure out a path forward.  Hopefully something like this will do:

   meson setup build -Dmode=minimal -Dlink-udev-shared=false -Dboot=true \
    -Dcore=false -Dnetworkd=false -Dresolved=false -Dhomed=false \
    -Duserdb=false -Dtimedated=false -Dlocaled=false

   ninja -C build && ninja -C build install

   Keep in mind, we've got at least one initramfs prog that links against the
   host system's libsystemd.so and also libnss_systemd.so, so we'll have to
   watch out for library mismatches... :-/

 - syslinux looks dead as a doornail as well.  that's sad.  there do seem to be
   some patched versions in distros, that may support dual EFI/BIOS booting via
   syslinux/isolinux... dunno if that's worth investigating.  I can't imagine
   using anything other than extlinux for BIOS systems, but we shuld probably
   start playing with systemd-boot for EFI booting

   I've played around with systemd-boot a bit.  Config is broken up into lots
   of little files instead of one, which seems annoyingly
   overcomplicated... but I suppose I can get used to it.  At least it's a
   project with a future.  It does not, however, support booting the xen.gz
   multiboot binary... which is unfortunate, becuase getting xen.efi to boot
   can be a PITA on some systems (e.g., I've actualy never gotten it to boot on
   my Threadripper box).  It looks like grub is the only mainstream, maintained
   bootloader that will do that...

   I've done some playing with grub now after decades of ignoring its annoying
   distro auto-gen config file nonsense.  I was able to quite easily get xen.gz
   booted via multiboot2 and xen.efi via chainloader, so that does offer an
   obvious standard boot approach... but I loathe the grub-mkconfig setup.
   It's trying way too hard to do everything for everyone and as a result just
   does it poorly.  I'll probably generate a hand updatable custom grub.cfg and
   attempt to disable update-grub and grub-mkconfig (although that will surely
   get undone by distro updates, maybe I can add a grub.d rule to just bomb out
   really early?)

 - maybe play with erofs to see how it compairs to squashfs?

 - ruckusrd --get-libdir so userland scripts can use ruckusrd-common.sh w/out
   having to hardcode its intallation path?  like what...?  do we have userland
   scripts that need this?  firstboot_wizard, linuxrc, mount_sysroot,
   mount_sysroot_vfs, system_installer, and umount_sysroot all source it from
   inside the initramfs, but it's guaranteed to live at /ruckusrd-common.sh in
   there.  If we had *real* userland usage, this comment would make sense.  And
   I don't think we'd *want* that file sourced by scripts post-boot anyway.

 - can i have the firstboot wizard ensure network connectivity and than run
   through a domain joining dialog?  domain name, username, password?

   not really...  firstboot runs before NetworkManager, so networking actually
   doesn't come up until later.  i suppose we could store domain, user, and
   password and run something during system bootup?  inject a
   ruckusrd-installer service to do it after Networking is up?  we' would have
   to temporarliy store a password somewhere...  seems a bit wonky, really.

   i'd file this away with non-trivial nice-to-haves, like automatically
   populating awesome-powers/config symlinks based on hostname...


