   i feel like nfs should be working, but when i try it hangs forever.  i can
   see the server has authenticated the mount request... it just never
   finishes?  i'm having strange networking behavior on this test, so perhaps
   i'll reboot and try again.

   that may have been because lo was down.  with it up i imediately get
   "Connection refused", even though i can see the server has granted me
   permission.  dmesg shows 'svc:failed to register lockdv1 RPC service (error
   111)'.  ok, i can '-o nolock' but perhaps that's bad.  otherwise i have to
   start the rpc.statd (and rpcbind?) sevice.  using -o nolock is fine for ro
   mounts.

 - Why can't I configure EFI bootloader when installing on Minisforum MS1-01?
   Is it a syslinux bug, or an efibootmgr bug, or just a bug in the EFI
   firmware itself?  I can install grub just fine after booting up off rescue
   media, and it works... i haven't tried configuring syslinux again post-boot,
   but it sure didn't work when done via the initramsys_installer... so could
   be something borky in our version of efibootmgr?  We are using an ancient
   version of efibootmgr, because of bionic build problems... might be time to
   finally disown bionic :-/

 - abandon bionic support and upgrade dropbear, efibootmgr, groff, fsarchiver,
   and enable rsync manpage.  can also abandon bionic support in firmwarenator
   and use zstd there (and upgrade firwarenator version in ruckusrd).

 - I've seen an ash error a couple times, perhaps just on really old debian
   boxes?  The host os shouldn't matter, though, it's coming out of initramfs.

   ash: .: unknown operand

   But then it boots fine...?  Definately saw it with my 6.18.44 kernel package
   w/ initrd built with v0.22.3... oh and that initrd doesn't have any
   microcode in it (I built it on feanor, I think... which must not have
   ucode.img in /boot).  I wonder if the lack of microcode is somehow related
   to the ash error...?


* proposed for 0.23

 - try and make sure ucode.img actually gets installed... i keep forgetting to
   do it on my kernel build box, so while i'm developing and creating initrd in
   the src dir, i get new firmware... but one installing ruckusrd via apt it's
   going back to old firmware.

   maybe i should make it complain if /boot/ucode.img is missing or older than
   6 months?  or older than the ruckusrd release date?

 - Creating custom fw.sqsh during install, then assuming it's ALL the firmware
   the kernel will need post-boot, and mounting it at /lib/firmware, can result
   in missing firmware for later hot-plugged devices.  Like network devices
   that don't initialize until they've got link... same is probably true for
   some video card firmware.  to fix this corner-case... we'll have to replace
   the small fw.sqsh with a giant one?  then boot up and fully initialize
   everything we need and recreate small fw.sqsh?

   Was this just a bad assumption to begin with?  The leaving fw.sqsh mounted
   part, I mean...  The original intent of FWDEV was to make sure early-loaded
   modules early-loading firmware would work...  once we hand off to init,
   shouldn't we let the remaining fw live on rootfs?  We could *inject*
   initrd-stage fw into rootfs, to make sure it's there, too, but even that
   might be a stretch?

   Does mod_inject cause fw injection at all, or just kernel modules and
   headers?

 - Migrate system_installer to grub, perhaps only experimentally?  could do a
   boot flag to control it... but we may as well just jump in with 2 feet.
   extlinux is bios only, syslinux only sorta works efi, and the project has
   been abandoned by its maintainer for years.

   This will mean we no longer need a "larger than usual" ESP, and that we can
   use a propper filesystem for /boot (so symlinks will work again).  Probably
   still want /boot to be a different ZFS dataset, so it can have different
   snapshots than rootfs.
